{"byteLength":471,"contentHash":{"algorithm":"sha256","value":"f6762f8d30455603492cc296884ca8487fca035593ab629bc12d76fc9c49c7c6"},"kind":"skillDocument","name":"references/stdlib/Pair.md","producer":{"build":{"commit":"5de2594ad523fba4f38fa63f7a1b306e8a23eb0c","dirty":false,"provenance":{"commit":"git","dirty":"environment"},"target":"aarch64-apple-darwin"},"name":"benitoite","version":"0.0.13"},"schemaVersion":1,"text":"# Benitoite.Pair\n\nStatus: standard, in the prelude. It can be used without `import`.\n\nRefer to its declarations as `Pair.<name>`, for example `Pair.Pair`.\n\nPairs of two values.\n\n```benitoite\npublic data Pair[A, B]\n  Pair(A, B)\nend data\n```\n\nA pair of two values, written `Pair(a, b)`.\n\n```benitoite\npublic function first[A, B](p: Pair[A, B]) -> A\n```\n\nReturns the first value.\n\n```benitoite\npublic function second[A, B](p: Pair[A, B]) -> B\n```\n\nReturns the second value.\n"}
